 "speed": 0.82,
 "text": "Your CPU can do a billion things a second. Fetching from memory is a hundred times slower. So each chip keeps a small, fast copy of the data it's about to need \u2014 and that copy is the cache. When the CPU asks for a byte, it looks in the cache first. Most of the time it's there: a hit. Sometimes it isn't: a miss, and the byte has to come from slow memory. So the cache throws out whatever it hasn't touched in the longest time, and keeps the rest feeling instant.",
